In Spring and Spring Boot web applications, handling HTTP requests requires defining component classes that map incoming web requests to specific methods. At the core of this mapping mechanism sit the stereotypes @Controller and @RestController. Understanding the precise distinction between them is vital for designing robust web applications and microservices.

Historically, Spring MVC relied entirely on @Controller, a specialized specialization of @Component, designed to support the Model-View-Controller design pattern. When a method inside a traditional @Controller returns a String, Spring's DispatcherServlet interprets that return value as a logical view name (such as a JSP, Thymeleaf, or FreeMarker template) and resolves it to render an HTML page. However, modern system architectures heavily rely on decoupled client-side single-page applications (SPAs) or microservices communicating strictly via JSON or XML payloads rather than server-rendered HTML. To eliminate the repetitive boilerplate of annotating every single method with @ResponseBody, the Spring team introduced @RestController in Spring 4.0.

@RestController is a convenience stereotype annotation that is itself meta-annotated with both @Controller and @ResponseBody. By combining these two annotations, @RestController tells the Spring container that every handler method within the class should automatically serialize its return value directly into the HTTP response body (using configured HttpMessageConverters like Jackson for JSON), bypassing view resolution entirely. Consequently, whether you are building a reactive WebFlux service or a traditional servlet-based Spring MVC API, using @RestController ensures your endpoints cleanly and implicitly output structured data payloads directly to the wire.

package com.example.demo.controller;

import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RequestMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/users")
public class UserRestController {

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public ResponseEntity<UserDto> getUserById(@PathVariable Long id) {
        UserDto user = new UserDto(id, "Jane Doe", "jane.doe@example.com");
        return ResponseEntity.ok(user);
    }
}
